# `todo/t0971` — an `Option[Option[Callable[T]]]` leaks its captured env: the
# OUTER generic enum's drop fn is never emitted at all.
#
# ⚠⚠ THERE IS NO `match` IN THIS FIXTURE, AND THAT IS THE POINT. An earlier
# revision matched on the value, which CONFLATED this defect with a second one
# (`todo/t0972`, the moved-out binding a match arm never drops). That one is NOT
# `Callable`-specific, so the conflated fixture could not support this item's
# central claim. Reading the value with `.is_some()` touches no arm and isolates
# the drop-emission mechanism on its own.
#
# THE MECHANISM, read off the emitted C:
#   grep -c 'Option__Callable__GorgetClosure__drop'         -> 2   (inner: emitted, and correct)
#   grep -c 'Option__Option__Callable__GorgetClosure__drop' -> 0   (outer: DOES NOT EXIST)
# so the outer value's scope exit reaches nothing at all.
#
# ⭐ THE DISCRIMINATOR IS `Callable`, AND HERE IT IS EARNED. The heap-forced
# `String` twin of this exact program — `Option[Option[String]]` whose value is
# 42 characters (a 41-char literal plus a runtime-concatenated digit, so it
# cannot sit in the small-string buffer), `.is_some()`, no match — is
# ASan-CLEAN on C and LLVM, and `grep -c 'Option__Option__GorgetString__drop'`
# is 3 (declared, defined, CALLED). Same nesting, same shape, different payload:
# the outer drop is synthesised for `String` and not for `Callable`.
# ⚠ THE TWIN MUST BE HEAP-FORCED OR IT PROVES NOTHING. A short String literal
# never leaves the small-string buffer, allocates nothing, and reads CLEAN for
# reasons that have nothing to do with drop emission. That mistake is exactly
# how this item's first control was falsified.
#
# ⚠ NOT the materialization class (`todo/t0937`, closed): this program prints
# the CORRECT `true` on both backends. The value is right; the memory is not.
#
# INTENDED: prints true and is ASan-clean.
void main():
    Option[Option[Callable[int(int)]]] o3 = Some(Some((int x): x + 1))
    print(o3.is_some())
